<h2>How the dispute reached the Supreme Court</h2>
<p>The parties married in 2007; a daughter was born in 2009. The wife left in 2011, alleging the husband was not maintaining them, and the panchayat-mediated agreement of December 2011 followed. When the husband sued for divorce on cruelty in 2012, the wife sought interim maintenance under Section 24 HMA. The trial court awarded Rs 4,000 per month with Rs 10,000 litigation expenses in 2014. An appeal was dismissed as not maintainable, and the husband&rsquo;s civil revision before the Madras High Court succeeded in part in 2024: the wife was to return the tea estate and agricultural parcels, keep the house, receive Rs 1,50,000 towards past maintenance, and get Rs 4,000 per month — but only after handing over the land.</p>

<h2>What the Supreme Court held</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The arrangement&rsquo;s purpose controls.</strong> The properties were placed with the wife and daughter expressly so that their income would maintain them. Its interim character did not permit restoration to be ordered at the Section 24 stage; that question belongs to the main proceedings.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>An offer to pay is not a lever.</strong> The husband&rsquo;s willingness to pay maintenance if possession returned could not convert an interim maintenance application into a possession dispute. The wife&rsquo;s refusal had to be read in the context of the agreement.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Stale figures must be revisited.</strong> Rs 4,000 fixed in 2014 could not survive unchanged into 2026, with a teenager&rsquo;s education and upkeep now on the mother&rsquo;s hands. The Court fixed Rs 15,000 per month prospectively.</div>
</div>

<h2>The quantum discipline the judgment reinforces</h2>
<p>The decision is useful well beyond its facts, because it addresses two recurring quantum problems in maintenance practice:</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Problem</th><th>The Court&rsquo;s approach</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Wife holds potentially income-yielding assets</td><td>Capacity to yield is not yield. Actual income, cultivation expenses and ability to manage must be assessed on material, not assertions</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Old interim figures carried forward</td><td>Needs grow with time — particularly a child&rsquo;s. Courts must account for the passage of years when the application is finally decided or revisited</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Conditional maintenance offers</td><td>Maintenance is an obligation, not consideration for extracting concessions; conditions that defeat the protective purpose will not be enforced</td></tr>
</table>
<p>For Delhi practitioners, the parallel is immediate: where family settlements or memoranda place property or its income with the wife towards maintenance, any later attempt to claw the asset back through an interim-stage order — rather than in the substantive proceedings — now runs squarely against <em>B. Prema</em>.</p>

<h2>Practice pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Plead the purpose of the asset.</strong> If property was given towards maintenance, exhibit the agreement and frame the possession question as one for trial, not the interim stage.</p>
  <p><strong>Prove income, not acreage.</strong> The side asserting that assets generate sufficient income must bring accounts, lease deeds or yield records; rival assertions decide nothing.</p>
  <p><strong>Seek periodic revision.</strong> Where litigation stretches across a child&rsquo;s schooling years, apply for enhancement rather than letting a decade-old figure run.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises a judgment of the Supreme Court of India for general information. It is not legal advice; maintenance outcomes depend on the evidence of income, needs and conduct in each case.</div>
